<section id="rubric">
<h2>Scoring rubric</h2>
<p>Each provider gets an editorial score from 1 to 5: the weighted result of six criteria. The criteria are the questions a buyer asks when choosing a vendor, so they differ by kind of product. Identity verification, eKYC, KYC, liveness, age verification and KYB providers use the first rubric; AML screening and monitoring providers use the second. Travel Rule providers are still scored on our previous rubric until their own methodology is published. Know Your Agent products have a rubric of their own, <a href="#know-your-agent">set out below</a>.</p>
<table>
<caption>Identity verification and KYC: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Fraud defense</th><td>25%</td><td>Independent liveness test results (iBeta ISO/IEC 30107-3 level), independently tested injection attack detection, NIST face recognition listings and NFC chip reading. For KYB providers: registry checks, UBO discovery, company documents and ongoing monitoring.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Pricing and total cost</th><td>20%</td><td>Whether per-check prices are published, monthly minimums, and a free trial or free plan. A vendor that quotes only through sales scores 2 out of 5.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Coverage</th><td>15%</td><td>Countries and territories (180 or more counts as global), document types and checks that need no document, as stated by the vendor.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Platform breadth</th><td>15%</td><td>How many jobs one vendor covers: identity verification, KYB, sanctions screening, ongoing monitoring, case management, video ident, reusable ID and age checks. Specialists are measured on the modules of their own category.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Integration and time to launch</th><td>15%</td><td>Public API documentation, web, iOS and Android SDKs, a self-serve sandbox or trial, a no-code or hosted flow, and published support hours.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Trust and track record</th><td>10%</td><td>ISO/IEC 27001 or SOC 2 (more if we confirmed the certificate), government trust frameworks, data residency options, years operating, and security incidents or regulatory actions in the last 24 months.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<table>
<caption>AML screening and monitoring: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Screening data and match quality</th><td>25%</td><td>Named sanctions lists with a stated update frequency, PEP tiers and relatives, adverse media sources, configurable fuzzy matching, and ongoing re-screening.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Pricing and total cost</th><td>20%</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Investigation and audit workflow</th><td>15%</td><td>Case management, the reason for a match shown to the analyst, an audit trail, risk scoring or rules, and batch screening.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Platform breadth</th><td>15%</td><td>Screening, adverse media, transaction monitoring, case management, business screening and identity verification.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Integration and time to launch</th><td>15%</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Trust and track record</th><td>10%</td><td>As above.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Each sub-score comes from a fixed table of rules, so the same evidence always gives the same sub-score, and every review shows its breakdown. If we can't find public information for a criterion, the review says "Not publicly disclosed" and that criterion's weight is shared across the others. User ratings and any commercial relationship are never part of a score.</p>
<p>Grades: Excellent from 4.5, Great 4.0 to 4.4, Very Good 3.5 to 3.9, Good 3.0 to 3.4, Fair below 3.0.</p>
</section>

<section id="know-your-agent">
<h2>Know Your Agent rubric</h2>
<p>Know Your Agent (KYA) products check the AI agents that act for a customer: whether a session comes from an agent, which agent it is, which verified person or business stands behind it, and what it is allowed to do. Providers with a KYA product get a separate KYA score from the rubric below. It orders the Know Your Agent ranking only. Each provider's review keeps its overall score.</p>
<table>
<caption>Know Your Agent: criteria, weights and what we look at</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">What we look at</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Agent detection and recognition</th><td>20%</td><td>Detection of agents that do not declare themselves, verification of signed agents (Web Bot Auth, Visa Trusted Agent Protocol, Mastercard Agent Pay) or a registry check, a trust score with reasons, and agent reputation shared across customers.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Human and business binding</th><td>20%</td><td>Whether each agent is tied to a verified person, or through KYB to a verified business, whether the person is checked again before high-risk actions, and independent evidence for that human check, such as an iBeta ISO/IEC 30107-3 letter.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Delegation and permission control</th><td>20%</td><td>Permissions by type of action, spending limits and an expiry, revocation at any time, and a check against the permission before each action runs.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Credentials and interoperability</th><td>10%</td><td>A credential in a published format such as a W3C Verifiable Credential, the agent protocols supported (MCP, A2A, AP2, ACP, Visa TAP, Mastercard Agent Pay, Web Bot Auth, OAuth), and open specifications adopted by a standards body.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Audit and monitoring</th><td>10%</td><td>A log of every agent action with the person and permission behind it, protection against tampering, monitoring after onboarding, and export for disputes and regulators.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Availability, pricing and integration</th><td>10%</td><td>Whether the product is generally available or in an open beta, a published price or free tier, public developer documentation, and a sandbox, trial or SDK.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Trust and track record</th><td>10%</td><td>The provider's trust and track record sub-score from the identity rubric, unchanged.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A provider enters the Know Your Agent ranking only when its KYA product is launched and sold, an open beta or pilot included. A framework, a white paper or a launch planned for a later date does not count: we list those providers with their status until the product ships.</p>
<p>Most KYA evidence is vendor-stated for now. No lab test or certification scheme for agent verification exists yet, and the Know Your Agent ranking says so next to the scores.</p>
</section>

<section id="evidence">
<h2>Where the evidence comes from</h2>
<p>Every fact on a review carries one of four labels, so you can see how much weight to give it:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Independent:</strong> a test lab, a government register or a public benchmark, for example an iBeta test letter, a NIST evaluation or a certification register.</li>
<li><strong>Vendor-stated:</strong> the vendor's own website, documentation, trust centre or pricing page.</li>
<li><strong>User-reported:</strong> review platforms such as G2, Capterra and Gartner Peer Insights. We show these ratings separately; they are not part of our score.</li>
<li><strong>Hands-on:</strong> our own test of the product.</li>
</ul>
<p>Numbered sources at the end of each review link to the page we used and the date we accessed it. Prices show the month we last checked them, and third-party price estimates are marked as such.</p>
</section>

<section id="hands-on">
<h2>Hands-on testing</h2>
<p>Most reviews are currently based on public evidence. When we test a product ourselves, the review gets a "Manually tested" badge and a Hands-on test section with the test date and results. Reviews without that section have not been tested hands-on.</p>
</section>

<section id="freshness">
<h2>How we keep reviews current</h2>
<p>We aim to re-check each review every 90 days. The "Last verified" date changes only after we have actually re-checked the provider's pricing, coverage and certifications, not when we edit wording.</p>
</section>

<section id="ranking">
<h2>How rankings are ordered</h2>
<p>Category and country rankings are ordered by each provider's score. When two providers have the same score, we keep the order they had before the tie. Providers we list but haven't reviewed in full yet appear after the reviewed ones, without a score. A provider whose score is still on the previous rubric is left out of rankings until it is re-scored.</p>
</section>

<section id="limitations">
<h2>What the score does not measure</h2>
<ul>
<li>Public evidence can't show false rejection rates, completion rates, manual review rates, false positive rates in name matching or latency on your own traffic. Vendor figures use different methods and samples. Test these in a proof of concept on your own users.</li>
<li>We don't load-test vendor APIs, so a score says nothing about performance at production scale.</li>
<li>We only see public regulatory actions and disclosures, not private examinations.</li>
<li>A score is an editorial opinion, not a legal opinion. Choosing a high-scoring provider does not by itself meet a regulator's expectations for vendor due diligence.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="disclosure">
<h2>How we make money</h2>
<p>BeVerified may earn a commission when a reader signs up with a provider through a link on this site. Payment never changes scores or ranking order.</p>
<p>Some providers pay for the link to their website. Those links carry a visible "Sponsored link" label next to the "Visit site" button. Links to providers that don't pay are marked <code>rel="nofollow"</code>. A paid link doesn't buy a review, a score, an award or a position in a ranking: paying providers are checked against the same rubric and the same sources as everyone else.</p>
